        {
          "entity": 17592186405153,
          "attribute": ":question/problem",
          "value": "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the real locus ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12=0$",
          "source": ":agent/gpt-6-astra-ultra"
        },
        {
          "entity": 17592186405153,
          "attribute": ":question/worked-solution",
          "value": "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the locus and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2x+6\\cdot 0+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1+12 & =0+1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+12 & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x-1)^{2}=-11.$ Therefore, the locus has no $x$-intercepts.",
          "source": ":agent/gpt-6-astra-ultra"
        },
        {
          "entity": 17592186405188,
          "attribute": ":answer-field/choices",
          "value": "17592186405189",
          "source": ":agent/gpt-6-astra-ultra"
